Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Back to Modeling the Material Flow
Modeling Transport Systems
Model Transport Systems
You will use transport systems in a variety of ways in your simulation models. You might have to model a
simple conveyor that transports parts between two stations, as well as complex electric overhead monorail
systems or forklifts that transport parts from one location to another, as well as cranes and automated
guided vehicle systems.
For this task you can use the Conveyor, the Track and the TwoLaneTrack, which you find in the folder
MaterialFlow in the Class Library.
• The Conveyor 
  is an active material flow object that has a propulsion system of its own. It transports
passive parts that do not have a propulsion system of their own.
• The Track 
  and the TwoLaneTrack 
  are passive material flow objects without a propulsion system
of their own. They mainly serve as the route on which the active material flow object Transporter 
drives, either forward or in reverse.
When modeling your transport system keep these characteristics of Conveyor, Track and TwoLaneTrack in
mind:
• All three are length-oriented objects, i.e., Plant Simulation uses their own Length, and the MU Length
of the MUs, which you type into their dialog boxes to determine how many MUs they can hold at
Modeling Transport Systems
Plant Simulation Help 10-751
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

the same time. The point-oriented material flow objects on the other hand, such as the Station, the
ParallelStation, etc. do not use a length.
• You can create Controls and Sensors for them.
• Parts transfer from a point-oriented object to a length-oriented object in their entirety, i.e., they are
located on Conveyor and Track/TwoLaneTrack in their full length as soon as their front has moved onto
these objects.
• Parts transfer from one length-oriented object to another length-oriented object with the Speed which
you type into the dialog box of the length-oriented object. This is of importance, when you use
Entrance Controls.
• To model simple transport systems, you will use the active object Conveyor.
• To model cross-transfer systems, AGV systems, cranes, etc. you will use the passive objects Track or
TwoLaneTrack.
By activating Automatic Routing of the moving objects Transporter, Part, and Container you can make
sure that they always find their Destination Object on the shortest possible route.
Back to Modeling the Material Flow
Model a Transport System with Active Objects of Type Conveyor
Model a Transport System with Active Objects
When you model transport systems with active objects, such as stationary belt conveyors, roller conveyors,
etc., you will use the Conveyor. Below we outline how you can use the Conveyor.
You can use the Conveyor 
  to:
• Model a Simple Conveyor Between Two Stations
• Model an Accumulating/a Non-Accumulating Transport System
• Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
• Model a Multiple Gap or a Multiple Pitch Conveyor
• Model a Transport System with Passive Objects
• Model Transport Systems
Model a Simple Conveyor Between Two Stations
In this example we model a simple Conveyor between two Station.
In its most basic way, a transport system consists of a conveyor that moves parts between two processing
stations. In our example we inserted a Conveyor 
  between two Stations, SP1 and SP2. The MUs are
Model a Transport System with Active Objects of Type Conveyor
10-752 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

transferred from the point-oriented station SP1 onto the length-oriented Conveyor, which then transports
them to the point-oriented station SP2.
The Conveyor moves the MUs from the station SP1 with the Final Speed we enter into the station SP2.
Plant Simulation computes the Transport Time using the Final Speed and the Length of the Conveyor.
Speed, Length and Transport Time depend on each other. When you change Speed or Length, Plant
Simulation recomputes the Transport Time anew. Changing the Transport Time also changes the speed.
Back to Model a Transport System with Active Objects
Model an Accumulating/a Non-Accumulating Transport System
In this example we model an accumulating and a non-accumulating transport system.
You can use the Conveyor 
  to model an accumulating or a non-accumulating transport system between
stations.
Model an Accumulating/a Non-Accumulating Transport System
Plant Simulation Help 10-753
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To make the MUs accumulate on the Conveyor, select the check box Accumulating. This enables the
MUs to move front to end to each other, when the exit of the Conveyor is blocked.
• To make the MUs retain their distance to each other, i.e., make all succeeding MUs stop moving when
the preceding MU cannot exit, clear the check box to deactivate Accumulating.
As we want to apply the default behavior of the Conveyor, we do not change its Capacity of -1, which
stands for an infinite number of MUs. This way its capacity is defined by its own length and by the length of
the parts (MUs), which it transports.
In our sample we entered an Availability of 85 percent and a MTTR of 6:00 minutes for the two Stations,
SP1 and SP2.
Plant Simulation marks a failed failure profile red.
You can use an accumulating Conveyor to decouple stations. Then, the Conveyor behaves like a roller
conveyor that enables the MUs to move up to those in front of them, when the exit of the conveyor is
blocked.
Conveyor accumulating 
 Conveyor non-accumulating 
When you do not decouple the stations, the Conveyor behaves like a belt conveyor, meaning that the belt
stops when a MU blocks the exit of the Conveyor and restarts as soon as the MU at the exit has moved on
to the successor.
Model an Accumulating/a Non-Accumulating Transport System
10-754 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model a Transport System with Active Objects
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
In this example we model a fixed gap or a no gap Conveyor.
You can use the Conveyor 
  to model conveyors ensuring gaps between the parts moving on it. The gap
is the distance between the rear of the preceding part and the front of the succeeding part.
• The default setting of the Conveyor for the MU Distance is -1, meaning that the feature MU Distance is
deactivated and that the parts can move onto the conveyor as soon as they arrive from the predecessor.
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
Plant Simulation Help 10-755
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• A conveyor with no gap between the parts does not allow a space between them, when you type in a
MU Distance of 0.
• A conveyor with a fixed gap ensures that the space between the parts moving on it always is the same.
The fixed gap causes the conveyor to frequently stop though.
In the example below we typed in 0.5 as the MU Distance, which ensures a space of 0.5 meters
between the transported parts.
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
10-756 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• A conveyor with a Minimum Gap ensures that the space between the parts moving on it will not
become less than the minimum gap. This setting prevents the conveyor from stopping and ensures that
all parts exit the conveyor if it transports a certain number of parts.
In our example we selected the MU Distance Type > Minimum Gap and typed in 1 as the MU Distance,
which ensures a minimum space of 1 meter between the transported parts.
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
Plant Simulation Help 10-757
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• A conveyor with a Minimum Pitch determines the minimum distance between the front of the
preceding part and the front of the succeeding part. This setting prevents the Conveyor from stopping
and from waiting for new parts and enables it to run dry.
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
10-758 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• A conveyor with a Pitch sets the distance between the between the front of the preceding part and the
front of the succeeding part. This setting ensures that the conveyor stops and waits for new parts. It also
enables the conveyor to run dry.
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
Plant Simulation Help 10-759
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To force the Conveyor to keep the specified Distance between the MUs, even if the MUs accumulate on
the Conveyor, select Enforce MU distance.
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
10-760 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

When you clear Enforce MU Distance, the MUs accumulate on the Conveyor.
When running the simulation in our sample model, you’ll notice the continuous flow of parts on the
minimum gap conveyor and on the conveyor which does not use a MU Distance. The no gap conveyor
and the fixed gap conveyor on the other hand stop quite often, denoted by the waiting parts.
Model a Fixed Gap or a No Gap Conveyor
Plant Simulation Help 10-761
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

