Stop the Transporter at Its Destination
Here we demonstrate how to stop the Transporter at its Destination, make it wait for a Part to be
transported, and then make it start driving again.
Our finished model looks like this:
Insert the required objects:
• The SourceT produces a single Transporter.
• The SourceP produces an infinite number of Parts and passes them on to Station1, which processes it.
• The Transporter drives from Track2 onto the Track, accelerates, drives on to its first Destination Station1
and decelerates in front of the Sensor1 on the Track. There it waits until the Part is processed and
Station1 loads it onto the Transporter.
• The Transporter then accelerates again, drives on, decelerates in front of Sensor2 at Station2, waits
there and passes the transported Part to Station2.
Program the required actions:
• Define the properties of the Transporter in the Entrance Control of the SourceT:
@.XDim = 1
@.YDim = 1
@.StopAtDestination = true
@.AccelerationEnabled = true
@.Acceleration = 0.2
@.Deceleration = 0.2
@.Destination = Station1
Stop the Transporter at Its Destination
Plant Simulation Help 10-779
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Define the sensors at which the Transporter stops and waits on the Track.
The Sensor Control of Sensor1 looks like this:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean, BookPos: boolean
var station = ?.sensorID(SensorID).Destination
waituntil station.Cont /= void and station.Cont.Finished
-- Wait until the station's content is not empty and the station's 
-- content has finished processing.
station.Cont.move(@)
@.Destination = Station2
@.Stopped = false
Stop the Transporter at Its Destination
10-780 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The Sensor Control of Sensor2 looks like this:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean, BookPos: boolean
var station = ?.sensorID(SensorID).Destination
var part    = @.Cont
part.move(station)
waituntil part.Location /= @
-- Wait until the part's location is not empty, i.e., until a part 
-- is located there.
@.Destination = Station1
@.Stopped = false
See also
Stop at Destination
StopAtDestination [SimTalk]
Using Automatic Routing
Use Automatic Routing
In this sample model we demonstrate how to use automatic routing to ensure that the parts always reach
their destination object on the shortest possible route.
Using Automatic Routing
Plant Simulation Help 10-781
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

You can:
• Work with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
You find the respective settings on the tab Routing of the Transporter.
The Transporter finds its Destination along the shortest route.
• Work with Automatic Routing of Part and Container
You find the respective settings on the tab Routing of Part and Container.
The Part and the Container find their Destination along the fastest route.
Back to Model Transport Systems
Use Automatic Routing
10-782 Plant Simulation Help
