Working with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
Work with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
In this sample model we demonstrate how a Transporter automatically finds its destination object. As soon
as the destination object is assigned a reachable destination, Plant Simulation computes the route which
describes the way on which the Transporter reaches its destination. The Transporter finds its Destination
along the shortest route.
The Source named SourceTransporters creates Transporters, which wait for parts at the sensor of the
LoadingStation.
In the Sensor Control we set that the Transporter waits until the SourceParts has produced a part of type
MyPart. Once a part is located on the LoadingStation, this part is loaded onto the Transporter. Then we
determine the destination object of the parts, i.e., one of the unloading stations Unload1, Unload2, or
Unload3. When the Transporter has reached the destination object, the part will be unloaded and the
Transporter returns to the LoadingStation, where it is loaded anew.
We will demonstrate how to:
• Insert and Configure the Tracks
• Program the Control for Loading the Transporter
• Configure the Sources and the Transporter
• Program the Control for Unloading the Transporter
• Make the 3D Model Visually Pleasing
Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Back to Use Automatic Routing
Insert and Configure the Tracks
Before we start modeling, we create the objects that we need as we do not want to change the built-in
object classes.
We duplicated the Source, the Track, the Connector, the Transporter, the Part, the Method, and the
DataTable in their respective folders. Plant Simulation places the duplicated objects into the folder
UserObjects in the Class Library. We renamed the objects and added them to the toolbar User Objects
with drag-and-drop.
Working with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
Plant Simulation Help 10-783
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Now we model our route network on which the Transporter drives. As a part of the route network consists
of three route loops, we first create a loop, copy it, and insert it twice. Then we connect the upper Tracks
with Connectors.
We continue this until our route network is finished. Make sure that the individual route pieces are
connected!
Insert and Configure the Tracks
10-784 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Then we move the individual loops so that they look like this:
Insert and Configure the Tracks
Plant Simulation Help 10-785
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Next we configure the LoadingStation and the three unloading stations, Unload1, Unload2, and Unload3.
For this we renamed the respective route pieces as shown below. In addition we colored them on the tab
Curve > Color to better recognize them.
Then we create a sensor on the LoadingStation at which the parts are going to be loaded onto the
Transporters. Click the right mouse button on the route piece LoadingStation and select Create Sensor.
We selected these settings:
Insert and Configure the Tracks
10-786 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Finally we insert the two Sources that produce the Transporters and the Parts, the SequenceTable, and the
method unload. Our model then looks like this:
Insert and Configure the Tracks
Plant Simulation Help 10-787
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Work with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
Program the Control for Loading the Transporter
After we created the route network, the LoadingStation, and the unloading stations, we program the
Sensor Control for loading the parts onto the Transporter.
In the Sensor Control we define that the Transporter waits until the SourceParts has produced a part of
type MyPart. As soon as a part is located on the LoadingStation, it is loaded onto the Transporter. The
anonymous identifier ? designates the part that is assigned to the contents of the calling object.
Next we determine the Destination with the instruction @.Destination. To do so, we differentiate the
consecutive number of the Transporter, @.ID, using the modulo function (mod 2 or mod 3) for integer
remainders. This way each third part receives an identical Destination. This is one of the unloading
stations Unload1, Unload2 or Unload3. After the Destination is assigned, each Transporter is assigned the
method named unload as Destination Control, DestCtrl.
Our source code of the Sensor Control looks like this:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean
waituntil SourceParts.Occupied prio 1
SourceParts.Cont.move(@)
if (@.ID mod 2) = 0
   @.Destination := Unload2
elseif (@.id mod 3) = 0
   @.Destination := Unload3
else
   @.Destination := Unload1
Program the Control for Loading the Transporter
10-788 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

end 
@.DestCtrl := "unload"
Back to Work with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
Configure the Sources and the Transporter
In this step we configure the SourceTransporters, which produces the Transporters and the SourceParts,
which produces the parts.
• As we always want to assign a Destination to the Transporter in our model, we selected the MU
Selection > Sequence for the SourceTransporters and our SequenceTable as the required table:
We entered our LoadingStation as the Destination into the subtable Settings. Apart from that we use
the default settings.
