Working with Automatic Routing of Part and Container
Work with Automatic Routing of Part and Container
In this sample model we demonstrate how a Pallet (Container) automatically finds its destination by
using its destination object. As soon as the destination object is assigned a reachable destination, Plant
Simulation the route which describes the way on which the Pallet reaches its destination traveling on the
Conveyors.
The Part and the Container find their Destination along the fastest route.
The Source named SourcePallets creates Pallets, which wait for parts at the sensor of the LoadingStation.
In the sensor control we set that the Pallet waits until the SourcePallets has produced a part of type
MyPart. Once a part is located on the LoadingStation, this part is loaded onto the Pallet. Then we
determine the destination object of the parts, i.e., one of the stations Unload1, Unload2, or Unload3. We
inserted a sensor each into these stations. When the Pallet has reached the destination object, the part will
be unloaded and the Pallet returns LoadingStation, where it is loaded anew.
We will demonstrate how to:
• Insert and Configure the Conveyors
Working with Automatic Routing of Part and Container
Plant Simulation Help 10-795
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Configure the Unloading Stations
• Program the Control for Loading the Pallet
• Configure the Sources and the Pallet
• Program the Control for Unloading the Pallet
• Make the Model Visually Pleasing
Our simulation model looks like this:
Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Insert and Configure the Conveyors
Before we start modeling, we create the objects that we need as we do not want to change the built-in
object classes.
We duplicated the Source, the Conveyor, the Connector, the Container, the Part, the Method, and the
DataTable in their respective folders. Plant Simulation places the duplicated objects into the folder
UserObjects in the Class Library. We renamed the objects and added them to the toolbar User Objects
with drag-and-drop.
Insert and Configure the Conveyors
10-796 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Now we model our route network on which the Container travels. To simplify matters, we saved our model
for automatic routing of the Transporter under a new name.
Then we held down Alt, dragged the object MyConveyor in the folder User Objects in the Class Library
onto the object MyTrack and dropped it there. This replaced all instances of the object MyTrack with
instances of the object MyConveyor. This way we do not have to manually create the route network. As
we kept the changes to the instances of the Track, we now have to manually rename the individual route
pieces, for example from MyTrack1 to MyConveyor1, etc.
Next we configure the LoadingStation and the three unloading stations, Unload1, Unload2, and Unload3.
Then we change the sensor of the LoadingStation at which the parts are going to be loaded onto the
Transporters. Click the right mouse button on the sensor of the LoadingStation and select Open Sensor.
We selected these settings. As the Destination is the LoadingStation itself, Plant Simulation enters self.
Insert and Configure the Conveyors
Plant Simulation Help 10-797
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Finally we configure our Sources that produce the Pallets and the Parts.
Back to Work with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
Configure the Unloading Stations
For each of the three unloading stations—Unload1, Unload2, and Unload3—we create a sensor each in
their center at which the parts are going to unloaded.
As we only want to activate the sensor when the Pallet has the same Destination as the one we selected
in the Sensor, we select Only when Destination. The Destination of the action, unloading the parts in
our case, is the respective station. Plant Simulation enters self when we select the respective unloading
station.
As Control we enter our method named unload in each case.
Configure the Unloading Stations
10-798 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Work with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
Program the Control for Loading the Pallet
After we created the route network, the LoadingStation, and the unloading stations, we program the
Sensor Control for loading the parts onto the Pallet.
Program the Control for Loading the Pallet
Plant Simulation Help 10-799
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

In the Sensor Control we define that the Pallet waits until the SourceParts has produced a part of type
MyPart. As soon as a part is located on the LoadingStation, it is loaded onto the Pallet. The anonymous
identifier ? designates the part that is assigned to the contents of the calling object.
Next we determine the Destination with the instruction @.Destination. To do so, we differentiate
the consecutive number of the Pallet, @.ID, using the modulo function (mod 2 or mod 3) for integer
remainders. This way each third part receives an identical Destination. This is one of the unloading
stations Unload1, Unload2 or Unload3. After the Destination is assigned, each Pallet is assigned the
method named unload as Destination Control.
Our source code of the Sensor Control looks like this:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean
waituntil SourceParts.Occupied prio 1
SourceParts.Cont.move(@)
if (@.ID mod 2) = 0
   @.Destination := Unload2
elseif (@.id mod 3) = 0
   @.Destination := Unload3
else
   @.Destination := Unload1
end
Back to Work with Automatic Routing of the Transporter
Program the Control for Loading the Pallet
10-800 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

