Modeling an Automated Guided Vehicle System
Model an Automated Guided Vehicle System (AGVS)
You can use the resource objects AGVPool and Marker to model AGV systems in your installation in which
the AGV, starting at the AGVPool, covers the designated route along the markers you inserted. The AGV is
not bound to a permanently installed route network, but drives freely within the space of the installation.
Note
When you insert Markers into your model, make sure that the Markers are aligned. This prevents Plant
Simulation from computing unnecessary roundings between the Markers. Misaligned Markers might
occur if you inserted Markers into your model while Show Grid was off.
Note
To show or hide a Marker, click 
  Show Connections.
We show how the AGVs:
• Cover a Route Along Omnidirectional Markers
Modeling an Automated Guided Vehicle System
Plant Simulation Help 10-807
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Here the AGV does not drive across the inserted marker but turns in front of the inserted markers
according to the angle that Plant Simulation computed toward the next marker. The icon of an
omnidirectional marker inserted into a Frame looks like this 
 .
Set the route pattern which the AGV covers in the method setRoute of the Transporter:
var AGV : object := AGVPool.Cont
AGV.setRoute([M1,M2,M3,M4]) // sets the route
waituntil AGV.DestinationWasReached
AGV.DestinationWasReached := false 
// recognizes when the destination of the route was reached  
print "The AGV is ready for the next route."
• Cover a Route Along Directional Markers
Here the AGV drives onto the marker and then rotates toward the marker around the default angle of
0° in the direction of the next marker. The icon of a directional marker inserted into a Frame with the
default angle looks like this 
 .
Cover a Route Along Directional Markers with the Defined Angle
Here the AGV drives toward the marker at an angle of 90°, which we defined, crosses it, and leaves it at
an angle of 90°.
Model an Automated Guided Vehicle System (AGVS)
10-808 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The icon of a directional marker inserted into a Frame with an angle of 90° looks like this 
 .
• Cover a Route According to a Segments Table
Here the AGV covers the route according to the route segments which we set in a table. To do so, we use
the method setRouteSegments.
In addition, you can:
• Load Parts Onto an AGV and Rotate Them
• Fine-position an AGV
• Prevent AGVs from Colliding
