Modeling a Battery-powered Transporter, Extended
Model a Battery-powered Transporter, Extended
With the settings on the tab Battery you can model a battery-powered Transporter.
The Battery battery has to be charged when the battery charge is lower than the reserve charge of
the battery. During battery operation power consumption consists of the Driving Consumption and the
Base Consumption. Within the Charge Control of the Transporter we have to prevent that the battery
gets discharged all the way and that the Transporter stops because of that. For charging the battery the
Transporter requires Charge Current. The time recharging takes is the difference between the Capacity of
the battery and its current charge and the charge current of the charging station.
Once the Reserve Charge during driving or the battery capacity at the end of charging is reached, the
Charge Control of the Transporter is called. We can differentiate these two events in the Charge Control
with the value of the battery charge using the attribute BatCharge.
We will demonstrate how to:
Modeling a Battery-powered Transporter, Extended
Plant Simulation Help 10-847
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Model a Battery-powered Transporter, Extended
• Program the Sensor Control for Loading and Unloading the Parts
• Configure Battery-powered Operation of the Transporter
• Program the Battery Controls
The finished simulation model looks like this:
Compare Model a Battery-powered Transporter, Basic
Back to Model Transport Systems
Model the Material Flow
In our first step we model the material flow for the battery-operated Transporter.
We proceeded as follows:
• The Source constantly creates parts of type MyPart.
Model the Material Flow
10-848 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• These are then moved on to the Buffer. We use the Buffer with its standard settings.
• For the Track we created two sensors with the settings below. The parts are loaded onto the Transporter
at the Buffer and they are unloaded at the Station:
Make sure that the starting point and the end point of the Track are connected so that the Transporters
can drive around in a circle.
• We use the Station and the Drain with their respective standard settings.
• In addition we inserted a Track as the ChargingStation and another Track for the ParkingPosition of the
Transporter.
• In our Init Control we create a Transporter each on the Track and on the ParkingPosition.
// create two Transporters
.UserObjects.MyTransporter.create(Track, 8)
.UserObjects.MyTransporter.create(ParkingPosition)
Model the Material Flow
Plant Simulation Help 10-849
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model a Battery-powered Transporter, Extended
Program the Sensor Control for Loading and Unloading the Parts
We would like to load the produced parts from the Buffer onto the Transporter at the first sensor and
unload them from the Transporter onto the Station at the second sensor.
We programmed this in our Sensor Control as follows:
param SensorID: integer, Front: boolean, BookPos: boolean
// load parts at sensor 1 from the Buffer onto the Transporter
if sensorID = 1
   @.Stopped := true
   repeat
       waituntil Buffer.Occupied prio 1
       Buffer.cont.move(@)
       wait 3 // the loading time is 3 seconds
   until @.Full
   @.Stopped := false
// unload parts at sensor 2 from the Transporter onto the Station
elseif sensorID = 2 
   @.Stopped:=true
   repeat
       waituntil Station.Empty prio 1
       @.cont.move(Station)
       wait 3 // the unloading time is 3 seconds
   until @.Empty
   @.Stopped := false
end
